#pragma once

#include <I18n.h>

#include <cstdint>

class EpdFont;
class FontDecompressor;

/// The CJK UI faces: the characters of the Chinese, Japanese and Korean translations, attached
/// behind the built-in UI faces as fallback glyphs (FallbackFaceProvider), so a UI string in those
/// languages draws. Generated by lib/EpdFont/scripts/build_cjk_ui_fonts.py, one face per language
/// per CJK_UI_FONT_SIZES entry.
///
/// They ship DEFLATE-compressed (compress_ui_face.py): 97,132 B of flash for all three at 10 and
/// 12 pt where they took 175,916 B flat. Decoding a glyph costs a few milliseconds on the C3 and UI
/// screens do not prewarm, so a Chinese Settings page decoded on demand would add 100-200 ms to
/// every first draw. The active UI language's two faces are therefore inflated once into the
/// language pack slot, after its strings (I18n, FlashLangPartition), and served from there like an
/// uncompressed face: no decode, no RAM. The other two languages' faces are decoded per glyph,
/// which only the language picker's names and CJK book titles ever ask for. If the slot cannot
/// hold the faces, the active language decodes on demand too: slower, never wrong.
///
/// They cover the translations and nothing else. A book title, an author or a chapter name in a
/// script they lack takes its glyphs from the reader's TrueType font while one is loaded (it stays
/// loaded past the reader, for Home and the Library), at the CJK UI faces' em; a CJK character
/// nothing covers draws as a box.
class TtfFont;

namespace CjkUiFonts {

/// What the language pack slot carries for a CJK language: its faces, inflated. Register with
/// I18n::setPackExtension() before I18N.loadSettings().
LanguagePackExtension& packExtension();

/// Where the faces that are not in the slot decode. Before setLanguage().
void setDecompressor(FontDecompressor* decompressor);

/// Give `font`, a built-in 1-bit UI face of `points` size, the CJK faces. It takes the largest CJK
/// size not above its own, or the smallest: 8 and 10 pt draw CJK at 10 pt, 12 and 14 pt at 12 pt.
/// Repoints font.data at a RAM copy that carries the provider -- the generated table is const flash.
/// Call once per face at startup, before anything draws with it.
void attach(EpdFont& font, uint8_t points);

/// Search `language`'s faces first, so a Han character that both the Chinese and the Japanese set
/// hold takes that language's glyph form; for any other language the order is Chinese, Japanese,
/// Korean. All three stay attached whatever the language: the language picker draws every
/// language's name in its own script.
void setLanguage(Language language);

/// The reader's TrueType family behind the UI faces (nullptr: none), searched before the translations'
/// faces, or after the active UI language's own. Its faces at the CJK UI sizes are made the first
/// time UI text needs one. SdCardFontManager calls it when a set loads and before its native sizes go.
void setReaderFont(TtfFont* font);
bool hasReaderFont();

}  // namespace CjkUiFonts
